y decades it's been the  interview and it still really is the  interview the face-to-face interview  the fact-finders are face-to-face with  people they're taking notes  they can talk to different people and  corroborate their stories  they are there in the context of that  place they can look around them  use information around them to build the  story  that's all very important for the  verification part right  they can look at the witness and they  can read the witness  as a person you know look for cues and  tells  the way that any of us do when we're  interacting with someone else in  conversation to judge whether or not  they're telling the truth  but just as important as this  verification process  is the solidarity that's going on  in this moment okay that is just as  important to human rights witnessing  human rights facts finding  solidarity so that is a feeling of  mutual support  a feeling of shared goals right a  feeling of shared feelings  about what's going on and solidarity is  underpinned by a demonstration of trust  right you have to have trust first to  build solidarity  verification and solidarity they go hand  in hand  fact finders get more information about  a human rights violation the more trust  they can build  and the sometimes kind of hard-nosed  nature of verification the kind of  fact-building  is tempered and made worthwhile by the  solidarity built between civilian  witnesses  and professional fact finders not to  mention the fact that getting any kind  of justice for human rights is a very  long road  so you know it is something to sustain  you as you're trying to make social  change  so this has been the gold standard and  then  this is called really the sort of second  generation of fact-finding  um the first generation was much more  about documentary analysis and speaking  to experts  the second one was about field visits  and these interviews  and then the third generation came along  a few years ago sorry i forgot to give  you  